The arrival cap of 500 per year was maintained throughout the period, though it was periodically reviewed. The cap applied to all migrants, including [[Bayani]], but did not include Sartan colonial personnel dispatched by the crown. The Bayani community, established during the early colonial period, continued to grow through both migration and natural increase.

By 1683, the island's population of approximately 80,000 represented a density of approximately 24 per km² on the island's 3,278 km², approaching the limits of pre-industrial agricultural capacity. Imported foodstuffs supplemented domestic production, but population pressure on land and forest resources was an ongoing concern.

== Cultural fusion ==

The most significant development of the period was the emergence of a fusion culture blending Guaji and Sartan traditions. By 1683, the generation that remembered pre-colonial Guajicutea had largely passed. The majority of the population had been born under Sartan rule.

=== Language ===

Sartan became the dominant language of urban areas, trade, religion, and government. The colonial language policy requiring Sartan for religious and commercial activity, implemented since 1609, had produced two generations of Sartan-dominant speakers in the cities.

The Guaji language survived in rural areas, fishing communities, and among older generations. Rural Guaji communities maintained the language in daily life, though even there Sartan was known for trade and official interaction. By 1683, urban Guaji were typically bilingual or Sartan-dominant, while rural Guaji maintained the language with varying degrees of Sartan influence.

The [[Kingdom of Murani]] served as a reservoir for the Guaji language, where it was maintained with less colonial pressure under the Burawi protectorate.

=== Religion ===

The Sartan [[Petran Catholic]] Church maintained its monopoly on formal religious institutions. But Guaji spiritual traditions — the triad of sea, mountain, and sky; the practice of cremation; the lunar calendar; the veneration of caves and water — did not vanish. Instead, they were absorbed into a syncretic practice that came to be known as Guaji Catholicism.

The mapping of Guaji cosmology onto the Catholic framework had begun during the Burawi period (1538–1609), when the three powers were associated with the Trinity. Under Sartan rule, this syncretism deepened and became institutionalized at the popular level, even as the official church hierarchy resisted it.

Cremation remained a point of tension. The Sartan Petran Church, like its Burawi and Volisanian predecessors, pressed for Christian burial. Guaji communities maintained cremation where possible, particularly in rural areas. A hybrid practice emerged in some communities: a Catholic funeral rite followed by cremation rather than interment, with ashes kept or scattered rather than buried.

The lunar calendar, officially replaced by the solar calendar in the colonial period, survived in rural agricultural and fishing communities as a practical and cultural system. By 1683, it was largely a rural tradition, maintained by farmers and fishers who found it useful for tidal prediction, planting cycles, and storm anticipation, even as the solar calendar governed official life.

=== Literature ===

The period saw the emergence of the first written literature by Guaji-born authors in Sartan. These works — histories, poetry, and religious texts — represent the beginning of a distinct colonial literary tradition.

The most significant cultural event of the period was the transcription and translation of the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle into Sartan, circa the 1650s. The Kari Yabí — the oral and carved record of Captain [[Kari Buka]]'s northern voyage (1502–1514 CE) — had been preserved in oral tradition and in Bikumaku glyphs. Its transcription into Sartan marked the first time the old Guaji stories were written down in the colonial language, bridging the oral and written traditions and preserving the chronicle for a colonial-era audience that was increasingly Sartan-dominant.

Other literary works of the period included colonial-era histories of Guajicutea, religious poetry drawing on both Petran Catholic and Guaji spiritual imagery, and practical texts on agriculture, navigation, and medicine that combined Sartan and Guaji knowledge.

=== Material culture ===

Sartan building techniques — stone, brick, and tile construction — entered the colonial architecture of the three cities, mixing with Guaji wood and {{w|bahareque}} (wattle and daub) traditions. The urban landscape of Tabakurí, Waribá, and Barikú increasingly featured Sartan-style administrative buildings, churches, and colonial residences alongside Guaji-style structures.

Clothing fused Sartan and Guaji elements. Sartan textiles and tailoring traditions mixed with Guaji preferences for lightweight tropical garments. Food combined Sartan imports — including {{w|wheat}} products, olive oil, and wine — with Guaji staples of cassava, yams, [[Makú]], and fish. The result was a distinct colonial cuisine.

=== Naming ===

Guaji naming conventions increasingly adopted Sartan forms. The feudal family names with the -an suffix — Guanaran, Barikan, Karuban, and others — faded in urban contexts, replaced by Sartan-style given names and family names. In rural areas, older naming conventions survived longer, particularly among communities that maintained stronger Guaji cultural identity.

